## Solution

Let $p = 131$ (prime), so $\mathbb{F}_{131}^*$ is cyclic of order $130 = 2\cdot 5\cdot 13$. Fix a primitive root $g$. Every $a \in \{1,\dots,130\}$ equals $g^i$ for a unique $i \in \{0,\dots,129\}$, and every $n$ equals $g^j$.

**Reachability.** $a^b \equiv n \pmod{131}$ becomes $g^{ib}\equiv g^j$, i.e. $ib\equiv j\pmod{130}$, which is solvable in $b$ iff $d_i:=\gcd(i,130)\mid j$. Thus
$$f(g^j)=\#\{i:d_i\mid j\},\qquad g(g^j)=\sum_{i:d_i\mid j}g^i \pmod{131}.$$

**Expanding the sum.**
$$S=\sum_{j=0}^{129}f(g^j)\,g(g^j)=\sum_{i,i'} g^{i'}\cdot\#\{j\in\{0,\dots,129\}:d_i\mid j,\ d_{i'}\mid j\}.$$
Since $d_i,d_{i'}\mid 130$, setting $L=\operatorname{lcm}(d_i,d_{i'})\mid 130$, the count of multiples of $L$ in $\{0,\dots,129\}$ is $130/L$. Hence
$$S=\sum_{i,i'}g^{i'}\cdot\frac{130}{\operatorname{lcm}(d_i,d_{i'})}.$$

**Grouping by gcd value.** The number of $i$ with $\gcd(i,130)=d$ is $\phi(130/d)$. Let
$$A_e=\sum_{i':\gcd(i',130)=e}g^{i'}.$$
The set $\{g^{em}:\gcd(m,130/e)=1\}$ is exactly the set of elements of order $130/e$, whose sum is $\mu(130/e)$ (Möbius inversion on "sum of elements of order dividing $k$"$=0$ for $k>1$, $=1$ for $k=1$). So $A_e=\mu(130/e)$.

Substituting $d'=130/d,\ e'=130/e$ (so $\frac{130}{\operatorname{lcm}(d,e)}=\gcd(d',e')$):
$$S=\sum_{e'\mid 130}\mu(e')\underbrace{\sum_{d'\mid 130}\phi(d')\gcd(d',e')}_{T(e')}.$$

**Computing $T(e')$.** Divisors of $130$ with $\phi$: $1{\to}1,\ 2{\to}1,\ 5{\to}4,\ 10{\to}4,\ 13{\to}12,\ 26{\to}12,\ 65{\to}48,\ 130{\to}48$.

| $e'$ | $T(e')$ |
|---|---|
| 1 | 130 |
| 2 | 195 |
| 5 | 546 |
| 10 | 819 |
| 13 | 1570 |
| 26 | 2355 |
| 65 | 6594 |
| 130 | 9891 |

(For example, $T(130)=\sum\phi(d')d'=1+2+20+40+156+312+3120+6240=9891$; $T(1)=\sum\phi(d')=130$.)

**Möbius values:** $\mu(1)=1,\ \mu(2)=\mu(5)=\mu(13)=-1,\ \mu(10)=\mu(26)=\mu(65)=+1,\ \mu(130)=-1$.

$$S=130-195-546+819-1570+2355+6594-9891.$$
Positive: $130+819+2355+6594=9898$. Negative: $195+546+1570+9891=12202$.
$$S=9898-12202=-2304.$$

**Reduction mod 131:** $131\cdot 17=2227$, $2304-2227=77$, so $-2304\equiv -77\equiv 54\pmod{131}$.

**Sanity check** with $p=3$ ($p-1=2$): direct computation gives $S\equiv 2\pmod 3$; the formula gives $\mu(1)T(1)+\mu(2)T(2)=2-3=-1\equiv 2\pmod 3$. ✓

The remainder when $\sum_{n=1}^{130}f(n)\,g(n)$ is divided by $131$ is $\boxed{54}$.

